confidence(A =&gt; B) / base_rate(B)
How much better the rule performs relative to the market's base rate
Edge
confidence - base_rate
The net probabilistic edge of the rule
The biggest mistake a beginner makes is to focus only on confidence. If the rule has worked 3 out of 3 times, the confidence will be 100%, but that does not prove anything: the sample size is too small. Therefore, the rule must meet a minimum support threshold: for example, at least 30, 50, or 100 historical instances. Otherwise, the robot will start believing in random coincidences.
Why This Is Not Just an Ordinary Indicator
A standard indicator returns a single value: RSI, EMA, ATR, slope, signal, or color. An association rule system works differently. It looks at a combination of events. A single event may be weak on its own, but when combined with others, it becomes informative.
For example, an RSI oversold condition does not in itself guarantee a reversal. The price can remain oversold for a long time during a strong trend. However, if oversold conditions coincide with rising volatility, a move away from the EMA, slowing momentum, and a specific trading session, such a combination may reveal a statistical pattern. Association rules look for stable relationships, not just a single indicator.
In this sense, the approach is similar to the manual reasoning of an experienced trader. A trader rarely thinks in terms of a single number. He says, "The RSI is low, the price is far from the moving average, the ATR has increased, and pullbacks are common in the European session." Association rules translate this logic into formal frequencies and probabilities.
How to Turn the Market into a Set of Events
To use association rules, continuous values must be converted into discrete events. An RSI of 31.7, an ATR of 0.00124, and an EMA slope of 0.00018 are awkward to use in rules. But it is convenient to say: RSI_LOW, ATR_HIGH, EMA_UP. This is called discretization.
