Important: Events must be sufficiently coarse-grained. If the market is broken down into hundreds of rare conditions, each rule will occur too infrequently. Then the statistics will look good, but they will be useless. For the first version, it is best to use 8–20 stable events and rule lengths of 2–4 events.
Label: What Counts as a Successful Outcome
The consequent of a rule must be measurable. The simplest option is the direction of future price movement after InpRuleHorizon bars:
FUTURE_UP   = Close[shift - Horizon] > Close[shift]
FUTURE_DOWN = Close[shift - Horizon] < Close[shift]
In MQL5 series indexing, a newer bar has a smaller shift. Therefore, the future bar for a historical example is at shift − Horizon. If `shift` is greater than `Horizon`, the corresponding future bar has already closed and is available in the historical data. During training, the outcome is known; in live trading, only the closed bar at shift=1 is used, ruling out access to future data.
It is better to add a minimum price movement threshold in points. If the price has changed by 1–2 points over the time horizon, that is not a useful signal — it is noise. The InpRuleMinMovePts parameter allows you to filter out such cases.
