After this training, a table of rules is stored in the Expert Advisor's memory. Each rule can be evaluated based on the probability of a price rise, the probability of a price decline, support, lift, and edge. These are the numbers used in the trading filter.
Probability Theory: How Not to Deceive Yourself
In trading, it's dangerous to focus solely on raw percentages. If a rule has occurred 5 times and was followed by a price rise 4 times, the probability of a price rise is 80%. But statistically, that's a weak basis. If a rule occurred 200 times and was followed by a price rise 120 times, the probability is only 60%, but such a rule may be more reliable because it has more observations.
Therefore, the filter needs three safeguards:
Minimum support.
A rule is not used until it has been encountered at least InpMinRuleCount times.
Probability smoothing.
Instead of using the raw up/total ratio, it is better to use a smoothed estimate so that rare rules do not receive extreme probability estimates.
Comparison with the baseline probability.
The rule must outperform the baseline frequency of a price rise or price decline in the market.
The Laplace estimate is a convenient tool for smoothing:
P(up | A) = (up + alpha) / (total + 2 * alpha)
If alpha = 1, even a rule with 0/1 will no longer yield 0% or 100%. Its estimate will be more conservative. This is important because the Forex market is noisy, and random extreme estimates quickly lead to overfitting.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Smoothed probability of a price rise under the rule.             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
RuleProbUp(
const
SRuleStat &r)
  {
double
a = InpLaplaceAlpha;
return
((r.up + a) / (r.total + 2.0*a));
  }
//+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Baseline probability of a price rise across all historical data used for training.  |
//+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
BaseProbUp()
  {
double
a = InpLaplaceAlpha;
return
((g_baseUp + a) / (g_baseUp + g_baseDown + 2.0*a));
  }
Choosing the Best Rule for the Current Market
On the current closed bar, the Expert Advisor builds an event mask. It then searches for all rules whose conditions are contained in the current mask. For example, if RSI_LOW, PRICE_BELOW_EMA, and ATR_HIGH are currently active, then rules such as RSI_LOW, PRICE_BELOW_EMA, and RSI_LOW+ATR_HIGH apply.
The best matching rule can be selected based on its score. A simple scoring formula:
scoreUp = (P(up | A) - BaseP(up)) * log(1 + total)
The probabilistic edge is multiplied by the logarithm of the number of observations. This prevents a rare rule with an attractive probability estimate from outranking a more robust rule with adequate support.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Checks whether ruleMask is a subset of currentMask.              |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
IsSubMask(
int
ruleMask,
int
currentMask)
  {
return
((ruleMask & currentMask) == ruleMask);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Find the best probabilistic estimate of the current state.       |
//| Returns confidence: + for up, - for down.                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
GetRuleConfidence(
int
currentMask,
double
&confidence)
  {
   confidence = 0.0;
double
baseUp = BaseProbUp();
double
bestScore = 0.0;
bool
found =
false
;
for
(
int
i=0;i<g_ruleCount;i++)
     {
       SRuleStat r = g_rules[i];
if
(r.total < InpMinRuleCount)
continue
;
if
(!IsSubMask(r.mask,currentMask))
continue
;
double
pUp = RuleProbUp(r);
double
edgeUp = pUp - baseUp;
double
score = MathAbs(edgeUp) * MathLog(1.0 + r.total);
if
(score > bestScore)
         {
           bestScore = score;
           confidence = edgeUp;
           found =
true
;
       }
   }
return
(found);
  }
Here, confidence is not the probability itself, but the edge relative to the baseline frequency. If confidence is positive, the matching rule favors a price rise. If it is negative, it favors a price decline. This format is useful for a trading filter: a BUY signal passes when confidence is sufficiently positive, and a SELL signal passes when confidence is sufficiently negative.
Integration into a Trading Filter
Association rules are best used as a gating filter rather than as a standalone entry trigger. The primary signal may come from an RSI divergence, an SMC zone, a breakout, a pullback to the EMA, or any other trading logic. Rules do not open a trade on their own. They simply answer the question: do the statistics support the direction of the candidate signal?
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Association rule filter.                                         |
//| dir = +1 for BUY, dir = -1 for SELL.                             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
RulesAccept(
int
dir)
  {
if
(!InpUseRules)
return
(
true
);
if
(g_ruleCount <= 0)
return
(InpRulesFailOpen);
int
mask;
if
(!BuildMarketEvents(1,mask))
return
(InpRulesFailOpen);
double
conf;
if
(!GetRuleConfidence(mask,conf))
return
(InpRulesFailOpen);
bool
ok = (dir == 1) ? (conf >=  InpRuleThreshold)
               : (conf <= -InpRuleThreshold);
return
(ok);
  }
That results in double confirmation. First, formal trading logic identifies a candidate. Then the probabilistic layer checks whether similar states occurred in the historical data and which direction they most often led to. If the candidate's direction aligns with the statistical edge, the signal passes. If not, it is rejected.
