Validation: Where Self-Deception Most Often Occurs
Association rules are very easy to overfit. Simply adding too many events, using overly long combinations of events, or setting the minimum rule support too low is enough for the system to find attractive rules even in random noise. That's why we need honest validation.
The correct approach for a time series:
The older 70–80% of the historical data is used to build rules.
The newer 20–30% of the historical data is used only for validation.
During validation, rules are not recalculated or adjusted.
The comparison covers not only directional accuracy but also the trading system's performance: profit, drawdown, expectancy per trade, and losing streaks.
Bars must not be shuffled randomly as they would be in standard tabular-data tasks. The chronological order is important. Randomly mixing older and newer bars can cause market regime leakage: a rule appears to be tested on future data, but it has actually already seen similar periods during training.
For diagnostic purposes, it is helpful to log the best rules. Not for manual tuning, but as a sanity check. If the best rule occurred 51 times and indicates a 98% probability of a price rise, that is suspicious. If a rule has occurred 400 times and yields a 58% success rate compared to a baseline of 50%, that seems more realistic.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Print the best rules for diagnostic purposes.                    |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
PrintTopRules()
  {
double
baseUp = BaseProbUp();
for
(
int
i=0;i<g_ruleCount;i++)
     {
if
(g_rules[i].total < InpMinRuleCount)
continue
;
double
pUp = RuleProbUp(g_rules[i]);
double
edge = pUp - baseUp;
double
lift = (baseUp > 0.0) ? pUp/baseUp : 0.0;
if
(MathAbs(edge) >= InpRuleThreshold)
         Print(
"RULE mask="
,g_rules[i].mask,
" total="
,g_rules[i].total,
" pUp="
,DoubleToString(pUp,3),
" edge="
,DoubleToString(edge,3),
" lift="
,DoubleToString(lift,3));
   }
  }
How Association Rules Differ from a Markov Model and an MLP
A Markov model typically estimates transitions between states: the market transitions more frequently from state A to state B or C. An MLP attempts to learn the nonlinear relationship between features and a future label. Association rules fall somewhere in between: they do not construct a complex function, but they can identify stable combinations of conditions.
