The strength of the rules lies in their explainability. You can open the log and see that the BUY signal passed the filter because the current combination of events had occurred 184 times, a price rise followed in 61% of those cases, the baseline probability of a price rise was 51%, and the edge was 10 percentage points. This is very valuable for a trading bot: the filter does not seem like a black box.
Practical Entry Logic
In a real-world system, the order of the filters might be as follows:
The basic trading logic identifies a candidate: BUY or SELL.
The risk manager checks the limits, drawdown, spread, number of positions, and trading hours.
The HTF filter checks the direction on the higher timeframe.
Association rules check whether the current combination of events has a statistical edge.
If all filters approve the entry, the Expert Advisor opens a trade or adds a grid level.
It is best to place rules toward the end of the chain, because their purpose is to confirm a setup that has already been identified, rather than to look for trades on every bar. Using rules as the primary entry generator produces too many weak statistical indications. If used as a final filter, they remove some of the poor entries and make the system more conservative.
