At first glance, the numbers look even more impressive than in the previous backtest: 51 closed positions instead of nine, a net profit of nearly 130 USD, and a Sharpe ratio of about 3.94. But this is precisely where the same critical scrutiny discussed in the section on rule validation is particularly important: it exposes the problem even more clearly than before.
First:
the sample is larger, but still covers only a short period. 51 closed positions in a single calendar month is a step up from nine trades and provides a statistically more representative basis for preliminary conclusions. However, this period still covers only one month, rather than a full annual cycle with different market regimes, so it is too early to conclude that the edge is sustainable.
Second
: The Profit Factor is undefined because the Gross Loss is zero — not a single losing trade out of 51 positions. This is a direct consequence of the exit mechanics: positions are still closed only upon reaching a fixed target of 1 USD (the target in this run has been lowered from 2 to 1 USD), and there is still no protective stop. A position that moves against the system is not forcibly closed — it simply remains open until the price reverses in the desired direction or the test ends. The formal result of "0% losing trades" is not proof of an edge, but rather a design feature: in such a system, losses are almost never realized; they are simply deferred.
Third
: The range of holding times has increased sharply, indicating a hidden risk. The minimum holding time is 40 seconds, but the maximum is 84 hours (about 3.5 days), with a mean of 5 hours and 48 minutes. This means that some positions are held through prolonged drawdowns while waiting for a 1 USD profit. An equity drawdown of 89.20 USD looks tiny relative to a deposit of 300,000 USD (0.03%), but this reflects an artificially large deposit and a small position size of 0.1 lots, not the strategy’s actual robustness: with a deposit comparable to that of a real trading account, the same trade held through a drawdown for 3.5 days would produce a much larger percentage drawdown. The Recovery Factor of 1.46 and the Sharpe Ratio of 3.94 should also be interpreted with this caveat in mind: they are calculated based on an equity curve that, technically, has never recorded a loss, which means both metrics are overstated compared to what an account with a forced stop would actually see.
Before treating the result as confirmation of the idea, the same three gaps as before should be addressed, taking the new figures into account:
Extend the period and add a walk-forward test.
One month and 51 trades represent a significant step forward compared to nine trades, but are still not enough to draw a definitive conclusion. We need a run covering at least 1–2 years of historical data, split 70/30 between training and out-of-sample testing, as described in the section on validation.
Add a protective stop or a limit on holding time.
Closing positions only at a 1 USD profit target without any risk limit is a dangerous setup for live trading, and holding times of up to 84 hours directly confirm this: a losing trade has no defined limit in either monetary or time terms. Even a fixed stop-loss or a time stop after N bars will make the test more realistic and safer.
Check the stability of the rules during retraining.
In this run, InpRetrainRulesBars=20 — the rule statistics are recalculated significantly more often than in the first version, where the parameter was set to 250. It is worth comparing how much the set of “good” rules changes between these frequent retraining runs — if it fluctuates wildly, the apparent edge most likely reflects noise rather than a stable pattern.
The result of this run is as follows: from a technical standpoint, the Expert Advisor works as intended — it compiles, trades, keeps track of statistics, and closes positions according to the specified logic — and the sample size has increased from nine trades to around fifty. But zero losing trades out of 51 in the absence of a protective stop is not evidence of an edge; it is a direct consequence of the exit mechanics. Before moving on to a live account — or even a demo account with real money — a longer backtest with enforced risk controls and out-of-sample testing is needed; otherwise, there is a risk of mistaking a statistical artifact of the system’s design for a working system.
Pitfalls
Rare rules
. The most dangerous trap is a rule that was rarely encountered but yielded impressive statistics. A minimum rule support requirement is essential. With limited historical data, it is better to use fewer events and shorter rules.
Market regime change
. A rule may have worked well in a trending market but stop working in a sideways market. Therefore, the statistics need to be recalculated periodically using a sliding window. Old data is not always useful.
Too many parameters
. If you optimize the RSI thresholds, ATR threshold, time horizon, minimum price movement, rule length, and threshold all at the same time, you can easily overfit the system to the backtest results. Keep the number of parameters small and validate the system on held-out historical data.
Confusing probability with profit
. The probability of a price move in a given direction is not the same as profitability. A rule may predict the direction correctly more often, yet yield a small average profit and occasional large losses. Therefore, after probabilistic validation, it is essential to review the trading statistics: profit factor, maximum drawdown, expected payoff, average profit, average loss, and winning and losing streaks.
Look-ahead bias
. All events must be calculated using only data from the bar at shift and earlier bars. A label may use future data only during training on historical data. When processing the live bar, only the last closed bar (shift=1) is used.
Possible Extensions
Weighted rule voting.
Instead of choosing a single best rule, you can select several suitable rules and average their votes based on weights for support, lift, and recency.
Separate rules for BUY and SELL setups.
Instead of simply counting FUTURE_UP/FUTURE_DOWN, you can evaluate the success of a specific trading scenario: whether the TP was reached before the SL or not. This is closer to real trading than simply assessing price direction after N bars.
Rule recency.
Older observations can be given less weight. The system will then adapt more quickly to regime changes, but will become more sensitive to noise.
An ensemble with an MLP.
Association rules can be combined with a neural network: the MLP provides a continuous estimate of direction, while the rules provide interpretable statistical confirmation. If both filters agree, the entry is stronger. If they disagree, you can reduce the lot size or skip the trade.
A separate table of rules for each session.
This is especially useful for Forex. The Asian, European, and American trading sessions often behave differently. Instead of a single shared set of rules, you can maintain separate counters for each session.
Conclusion
We transformed the idea of “the market as a series of recurring states” into a practical MQL5 module: discretizing features into events, encoding them with a bit mask, collecting combination frequencies, and linking them to a label indicating future price movement. Important safeguards are built in by default: minimum support, Laplace smoothing, and comparison with the base probability. The reader gets ready-to-use interfaces: TrainRules collects statistics over a window of historical data; GetRuleConfidence evaluates the current mask in terms of edge/confidence; and RulesAccept(dir) serves as the final gate for BUY/SELL. Mandatory practices prior to live trading: dividing the historical data by time (70/30 training/validation), prohibiting bar shuffling, testing with real risk limits (fixed stop-losses or time stops), and extended backtesting across various market conditions.
Remember: rules are not a signal generator, but a confirmation filter; the criterion for readiness is stable rules with sufficient support and a consistent edge in validation. In short: formalize events and labels → calculate statistics and safeguard estimates → obtain the confidence estimate for the current bar → apply RulesAccept(dir).
